## 6. What you gain with one group per topic

| Property | Effect |
|---|---|
| **Independent offsets / lag** | Kafka lag metrics (`kafka_consumergroup_lag`) are per business → clear alerting |
| **Independent rebalances** | Scaling pods or a slow topic only rebalances *that* group |
| **Fault isolation** | `retryWhen` restarts a single stream; other topics keep flowing |
| **Per-topic tuning** | `max.poll.records`, `auto.offset.reset`, concurrency, or even a different cluster via `bootstrap.servers` override |
| **Selective pause/replay** | Reset offsets or `enabled: false` for one business only (`kafka-consumer-groups --reset-offsets --group loan-ncc-prod`) |
| **Security** | ACLs can be granted per group per topic |

### Two things to watch
1. **Thread footprint** — each `KafkaReceiver` runs its own poll loop, so N topics = N consumer threads. Fine for dozens; if you ever reach hundreds, group topics with identical SLAs under a shared group instead.
2. **Naming** — keep group IDs stable across deployments (never derive them from hostname or a random suffix), otherwise you'll reprocess from `auto.offset.reset` on every deploy. The `CLIENT_ID` (not group) is where the hostname goes, as shown above.

Everything else from the previous design (handlers, Redis lookup, resilient API client, DLT, ordering per partition) stays exactly the same.